::ENG4_U03_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A: A city report says weekend library hours should expand because 63 percent of survey respondents said they need quiet study space after work. Passage B: A student letter says weekend library hours should expand because students deserve a safe place to study instead of trying to finish assignments in noisy homes. Which comparison best evaluates how the two arguments use information?
{
~A. Passage A is weaker because it uses numbers instead of describing student feelings.#Incorrect
=B. Passage A uses survey data to build a policy case, while Passage B uses student experience to build urgency and empathy.#Correct
~C. Passage B is stronger because emotional language is always more persuasive than data.#Incorrect
~D. Both passages make the same claim, so their evidence has the same effect.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A school board memo argues for later bus pickup times by focusing on transportation data, route efficiency, and implementation costs. What author purpose is most likely shaping this rhetorical approach?
{
=A. To persuade decision-makers by presenting the proposal as practical and manageable.#Correct
~B. To entertain readers with a dramatic story about morning routines.#Incorrect
~C. To confuse readers by using technical information instead of examples.#Incorrect
~D. To avoid making a claim about bus pickup times.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Passage: An article argues that community gardens improve neighborhoods. It cites reduced vacant lots, increased volunteer participation, and lower summer grocery costs for participating families. Which statement best evaluates the support?
{
~A. The support is ineffective because it includes more than one kind of evidence.#Incorrect
~B. The support is unrelated because gardens and grocery costs are different topics.#Incorrect
=C. The support is effective because it connects physical, social, and economic benefits to the central idea.#Correct
~D. The support is effective only because it includes statistics.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: One writer describes a proposed policy as a "temporary pilot," while another calls it a "necessary reform." What does the difference in wording most likely show?
{
~A. Both writers are trying to avoid taking a position.#Incorrect
~B. The writers disagree about every fact in the issue.#Incorrect
~C. The second writer uses less precise language than the first writer.#Incorrect
=D. The writers frame the same policy differently, one cautiously and one urgently.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Passage A: The editorial argues that schools should replace disposable trays because landfill costs rose by 14 percent. Passage B: The editorial argues that schools should replace disposable trays because students should not learn environmental responsibility from a wasteful cafeteria. Which answer best evaluates the stronger argument for a budget committee?
{
~A. Passage B is stronger because it uses values instead of money.#Incorrect
=B. Passage A is stronger for that audience because it connects the proposal to documented financial impact.#Correct
~C. Passage A is weaker because it does not mention student responsibility.#Incorrect
~D. Passage B is stronger because its tone is more forceful.#Incorrect
}
